{"id":99,"date":"2026-07-09T14:25:53","date_gmt":"2026-07-09T14:25:53","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/localhost:8181\/is-it-time-to-replace-that-aging-air-conditioner-in-your-easton-home\/"},"modified":"2026-07-14T19:43:54","modified_gmt":"2026-07-14T19:43:54","slug":"is-it-time-to-replace-that-aging-air-conditioner-in-your-easton-home","status":"publish","type":"page","link":"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/is-it-time-to-replace-that-aging-air-conditioner-in-your-easton-home\/","title":{"rendered":"14-Foot Ceilings and Brick Walls \u2014 Heating an Easton Loft Conversion"},"content":{"rendered":"\n<div class=\"wp-block-media-text alignfull has-media-on-the-right s-hero-split wp-elements-dd19bbe8661a714827225cda31fd96dc\" style=\"grid-template-columns:auto 50%\"><div class=\"wp-block-media-text__content\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading s-hero-split__text wp-block-heading\">Industrial comfort, no compromise<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The Lehigh Valley&#039;s mill buildings spent a century making silk, cement, and steel, and now they&#039;re being gutted for loft apartments where 14-foot ceilings, exposed ductwork, and brick walls become selling points\u2014but the HVAC systems that feed them are anything but ornamental.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-buttons is-content-justification-center is-layout-flex wp-container-core-buttons-is-layout-35f06ea7 wp-block-buttons-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-button\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"\/contact\/\">Request a Quote<\/a><\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div><figure class=\"wp-block-media-text__media\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"683\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-ductwork-1-1024x683.png\" alt=\"ductwork - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1478\" style=\"object-fit:cover;height:100%\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-ductwork-1-1024x683.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-ductwork-1-300x200.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-ductwork-1-768x512.png 768w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-ductwork-1.png 1536w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gpt-image-1)\">AI<\/span><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"s-trust-bar\"><div class=\"s-trust__item\">15+ Years Serving PA<\/div><div class=\"s-trust__item\">Licensed &amp; Insured<\/div><div class=\"s-trust__item\">Same-Day Emergency<\/div><div class=\"s-trust__item\">Local to Your Area<\/div><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-columns alignwide s-stats is-layout-flex wp-container-core-columns-is-layout-794e3cfa wp-block-columns-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-stats__item is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<p class=\"s-stats__number has-x-large-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">18-24&quot;<\/p>\n\n<p class=\"s-stats__label wp-block-paragraph\">Brick wall thickness\u2014thermal mass that delays heating response<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-stats__item is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<p class=\"s-stats__number has-x-large-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">15-20%<\/p>\n\n<p class=\"s-stats__label wp-block-paragraph\">Energy loss in uninsulated exposed ductwork over long runs<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-stats__item is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<p class=\"s-stats__number has-x-large-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">2-3 hrs<\/p>\n\n<p class=\"s-stats__label wp-block-paragraph\">Lag between cooling system start and full wall temperature drop<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-stats__item is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<p class=\"s-stats__number has-x-large-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">30%<\/p>\n\n<p class=\"s-stats__label wp-block-paragraph\">Potential energy savings with smart thermostat + zoning<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--sand has-tint-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-media-text has-media-on-the-right\" style=\"grid-template-columns:auto 50%\"><div class=\"wp-block-media-text__content\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Rooftop units, vertical distribution<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Most Easton mill-to-loft conversions use rooftop HVAC units rather than basement or mechanical-room equipment. The buildings often lack interior space suitable for central plants, and rooftop placement keeps equipment out of sight. Each rooftop unit serves one or more residential units, with ductwork dropping vertically through existing stairwell chases, elevator shafts, or newly created vertical shafts that double as service corridors for plumbing and electrical.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The advantage of rooftop units is that they&#039;re out of the way\u2014no equipment noise in living spaces, no basement footprint, no outdoor condensers cluttering courtyards or sidewalks. The disadvantage is serviceability. When a rooftop unit fails in January, the technician has to access the roof in snow and wind. Insulation and vapor barriers on vertical duct runs are non-negotiable in Easton&#039;s climate.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Winter air at 120\u00b0F traveling through uninsulated ductwork in a cold stairwell chase will lose 30\u00b0F or more by the time it reaches lower floors. Wrapping ductwork with faced fiberglass insulation (R-6 minimum) and sealing all joints with mastic keeps the heat where it belongs and prevents condensation during summer cooling.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Some contractors skip insulation on ductwork that runs through conditioned space, but in a loft with 14-foot ceilings, the upper 4 feet often stratifies\u2014warmer in winter, cooler in summer\u2014so even interior ductwork benefits from insulation.<\/p>\n\n<\/div><figure class=\"wp-block-media-text__media\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-condenser-1.png\" alt=\"condenser - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1767\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-condenser-1.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-condenser-1-300x300.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-condenser-1-150x150.png 150w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-condenser-1-768x768.png 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gemini-2.5-flash-image)\">AI<\/span><\/figure><\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--sand has-tint-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">HVAC solutions for loft living<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-columns alignwide s-feature-row is-layout-flex wp-container-core-columns-is-layout-794e3cfa wp-block-columns-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-feature-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-medium s-feature-card__img\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-ductwork-1.png\" alt=\"ductwork - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1759\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-ductwork-1.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-ductwork-1-300x300.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-ductwork-1-150x150.png 150w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-ductwork-1-768x768.png 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gemini-2.5-flash-image)\">AI<\/span><\/figure>\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Exposed duct design<\/h3>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Raw galvanized, painted, or metal-clad ductwork integrated into your ceiling aesthetic. Every joint sealed, every seam architectural.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-feature-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-medium s-feature-card__img\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-1.png\" alt=\"technician - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1755\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-1.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-1-300x300.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-1-150x150.png 150w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-1-768x768.png 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gemini-2.5-flash-image)\">AI<\/span><\/figure>\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Rooftop equipment<\/h3>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Quiet, out-of-sight units that don&#039;t claim valuable interior floor space. Vertical distribution through shaft systems keeps runs compact.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-feature-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-medium s-feature-card__img\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-furnace-1.png\" alt=\"furnace - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1757\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-furnace-1.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-furnace-1-300x300.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-furnace-1-150x150.png 150w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-furnace-1-768x768.png 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gemini-2.5-flash-image)\">AI<\/span><\/figure>\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Thermal mass heating<\/h3>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Smart thermostats that anticipate occupancy and preheat brick walls. Radiant floor systems for supplemental base-load comfort.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-feature-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-medium s-feature-card__img\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-minisplit-1.png\" alt=\"minisplit - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1765\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-minisplit-1.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-minisplit-1-300x300.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-minisplit-1-150x150.png 150w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-minisplit-1-768x768.png 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gemini-2.5-flash-image)\">AI<\/span><\/figure>\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Zone control<\/h3>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Independent temperature management for kitchen, bedroom, and living areas. Damper-based or ductless mini-split options.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-feature-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-medium s-feature-card__img\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-ductwork-2.png\" alt=\"ductwork - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1760\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-ductwork-2.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-ductwork-2-300x300.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-ductwork-2-150x150.png 150w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-ductwork-2-768x768.png 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gemini-2.5-flash-image)\">AI<\/span><\/figure>\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Fresh air ventilation<\/h3>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Mechanical ventilation meeting code without energy waste. Energy recovery ventilators reclaim heat from exhaust air.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-feature-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-medium s-feature-card__img\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-2.png\" alt=\"technician - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1756\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-2.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-2-300x300.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-2-150x150.png 150w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-2-768x768.png 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gemini-2.5-flash-image)\">AI<\/span><\/figure>\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">High-efficiency filtration<\/h3>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">4-inch pleated media filters capture dust and particulates from exposed brick, reclaimed wood, and polished concrete.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--cream has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-media-text\" style=\"grid-template-columns:50% auto\"><figure class=\"wp-block-media-text__media\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"683\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-thermostat-1-1024x683.png\" alt=\"thermostat - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1479\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-thermostat-1-1024x683.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-thermostat-1-300x200.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-thermostat-1-768x512.png 768w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-thermostat-1.png 1536w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gpt-image-1)\">AI<\/span><\/figure><div class=\"wp-block-media-text__content\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Heating thick walls, managing thermal lag<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The 18- to 24-inch masonry walls in Easton&#039;s mill buildings have tremendous thermal mass\u2014they absorb heat slowly and release it slowly, creating a lag between when the HVAC system runs and when the space reaches comfortable temperature. In winter, the walls act as a heat sink.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">You crank the thermostat to 72\u00b0F, the forced-air system runs, and the air temperature hits 72\u00b0F within 20 minutes, but the walls are still 58\u00b0F because they haven&#039;t absorbed enough energy to warm up. The air near the walls feels colder, and the thermostat cycles on and off repeatedly. The solution is a combination of continuous low-level heating and thermostat programming that anticipates occupancy.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Smart thermostats with learning algorithms are particularly effective in high-thermal-mass spaces because they learn how long it takes the space to warm up and begin preheating before occupants arrive. In a loft occupied from 6 p.m. to 8 a.m., the thermostat might start heating at 4 p.m. to have the space fully comfortable by 6 p.m., then maintain a lower setback temperature during the day.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">This strategy reduces heating demand by 20-30% compared to deep setbacks that allow the walls to fully cool. Radiant floor heating is occasionally installed as a supplemental system, heating surfaces rather than air. It&#039;s well-suited to high-ceilinged spaces where forced air tends to stratify. The downside is cost\u2014$12 to $25 per square foot installed\u2014and the fact that radiant heat is slow to respond.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">It&#039;s a luxury feature, not a necessity, but in a 2,000-square-foot loft with 20-foot ceilings, it makes a noticeable difference in comfort.<\/p>\n\n<\/div><\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--warm-gradient has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Zoning strategies for open-plan lofts<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list s-steps\">\n<li>Damper-based zoning \u2014 Single rooftop unit with motorized dampers in duct branches serving different zones. Each zone has its own thermostat; dampers open or close to direct airflow where needed. Works well for two or three distinct areas.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Multi-zone ductless mini-splits \u2014 Individual wall-mounted or ceiling-recessed heads in each zone, operating independently. Cool the bedroom at night while leaving the living area off. Quieter and more energy-efficient than zoned forced-air.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Dual-fuel hybrid systems \u2014 Radiant floor heat for base load combined with ductless mini-splits for quick-response cooling. Most expensive option but delivers highest comfort in large lofts with extreme ceiling heights.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Ceiling fan destratification \u2014 Reversible fans running in winter on low speed to circulate warm air from ceiling back to occupied zones. Reduces heating demand without HVAC changes.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Portable supplemental heat \u2014 Infrared heaters or portable AC units for localized comfort issues. Common in owner-occupied lofts where residents actively manage comfort.<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Ventilation and indoor air quality<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Old mill buildings in Easton were not designed for continuous occupancy. Converting them to residential use requires mechanical ventilation to meet code requirements for fresh air and to prevent indoor air quality problems. Pennsylvania&#039;s Uniform Construction Code requires 50 to 100 CFM of fresh air per dwelling unit.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The most common approach is to integrate fresh air intake into the HVAC system&#039;s return duct, using a motorized damper that opens when the air handler runs and draws in outdoor air through a screened and filtered louver. Energy recovery ventilators (ERVs) are a more sophisticated solution, especially in lofts with high heating or cooling loads.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">An ERV captures heat and humidity from exhaust air and transfers it to incoming fresh air, reducing the energy penalty of ventilation. In winter, the ERV preheats incoming outdoor air; in summer, it pre-cools and dehumidifies. The efficiency gain is significant\u2014ERVs can recover 70-80% of the energy that would otherwise be lost\u2014but the equipment costs $1,800 to $3,500 installed.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In Easton&#039;s industrial loft market, ERVs are standard in higher-end conversions and rare in budget-driven projects. Indoor air quality is affected by materials used in construction\u2014exposed brick can shed dust, reclaimed wood beams may off-gas VOCs, and polished concrete floors release alkaline dust. High-efficiency air filtration helps. Upgrading from the standard 1-inch fiberglass filter (MERV 1-4) to a 4-inch pleated media filter (MERV 11-13) captures particulates down to 1 micron.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Some loft owners install standalone HEPA air purifiers in sleeping areas. The purifiers aren&#039;t a substitute for proper HVAC filtration, but they&#039;re effective for localized air quality improvement.<\/p>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--ink has-contrast-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading has-text-align-center wp-block-heading\">What to expect: loft HVAC costs and performance<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-columns alignwide s-cards-3 is-layout-flex wp-container-core-columns-is-layout-794e3cfa wp-block-columns-is-layout-flex\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Installation cost<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">$15,000 to $30,000 for a 1,500-square-foot loft. Rooftop units with exposed galvanized ductwork are at the lower end; ductless systems or high-design painted ductwork push toward the higher end.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Energy efficiency<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Thermal mass moderates temperature swings. Not as efficient as modern construction, but smart thermostats, ERVs, and zoning can reduce consumption by 20-30%.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Ductwork finish options<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Raw galvanized ($0\/sq ft), painted ($2-4\/sq ft), or metal-clad ($30-60\/sq ft). Each affects visual integration and maintenance requirements.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Responsive comfort<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Expect 2-3 hour lag for walls to reach full temperature in winter. Zoning and smart thermostats minimize the effect. Mini-splits respond faster for quick warming.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Maintenance access<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Rooftop units require roof access in winter for service. Redundant units in multi-tenant buildings improve reliability. Plan accordingly.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Thermal performance<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Loft conversions need 1 ton of cooling per 400-500 sq ft of open floor plan. Oversizing doesn&#039;t compensate for thermal lag or wall stratification.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">From silk mills to smart thermostats<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Easton&#039;s industrial buildings are a defining piece of the Lehigh Valley&#039;s architectural identity. Their conversion to housing has revitalized neighborhoods along the Delaware waterfront and the South Side. The HVAC systems in these lofts aren&#039;t afterthoughts\u2014they&#039;re integral to making the spaces livable year-round. Developers who understand this invest in thoughtful mechanical design: exposed ductwork that looks intentional, zoning that matches how people actually use the space, and ventilation that keeps the air fresh without wasting energy.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list s-icon-list\">\n<li>Exposed ductwork is both practical and aesthetically consistent with industrial heritage<\/li>\n\n\n<li>High ceilings mean thermal stratification\u2014top and bottom zones need independent control<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Thick brick walls moderate temperature swings but require anticipatory heating<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Fresh air ventilation is essential in sealed lofts\u2014code-minimum approaches waste energy<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Smart thermostats and ERVs recoup 20-30% of system costs through energy savings<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Contractors specializing in adaptive reuse solve problems that don&#039;t appear in textbooks<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-quote s-quote--accent has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<figure class=\"wp-block-pullquote\"><blockquote><p>If you&#039;re buying or renting a loft in Easton, ask about the HVAC system&#039;s design, not just its tonnage. The difference between a thoughtfully designed system and a code-minimum installation is the difference between loving the space and fighting it every season.<\/p><cite>Keystone Comfort Co.<\/cite><\/blockquote><\/figure>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading has-text-align-center wp-block-heading\">HVAC in Easton&#039;s loft conversions: what to expect<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-accordion s-accordion--bordered has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-1583283a wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Why is exposed ductwork so common in Easton lofts?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Industrial loft conversions feature high ceilings\u201412 to 16 feet. Dropping a suspended ceiling to hide ductwork wastes vertical space and undermines the open warehouse aesthetic. Exposed ductwork is both practical and stylistically consistent with the building&#039;s industrial heritage. Properly designed and finished, it becomes an architectural feature rather than an eyesore.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>How much does it cost to install HVAC in a converted mill loft?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Expect $15,000 to $30,000 for a 1,500-square-foot loft, depending on system type, duct routing complexity, and finish level. Rooftop units with exposed galvanized ductwork are at the lower end. Ductless mini-split systems or high-design painted ductwork with custom diffusers push toward the higher end. Radiant floor heating as a supplemental system adds $18,000 to $40,000.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Are these lofts energy-efficient to heat and cool?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Not compared to modern well-insulated construction, but thermal mass helps. The thick brick walls moderate temperature swings\u2014lofts stay cooler in summer and retain heat longer in winter. Energy costs are typically higher than a comparably sized suburban home, but smart thermostats, ERVs, and zoning can reduce consumption by 20-30%.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Why does my loft never reach setpoint, even when the system runs constantly?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Thermal lag in thick masonry walls and stratification in high-ceilinged spaces prevent even temperature distribution. Oversizing the equipment doesn&#039;t help\u2014you need anticipatory thermostat programming and zoning. A diagnostic visit reveals exactly where airflow and temperature distribution are breaking down.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>What&#039;s the difference between rooftop units and ductless mini-splits?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Rooftop units are cheaper to install ($12,000-$18,000) and keep equipment out of sight, but they&#039;re harder to service in winter. Ductless mini-splits are quieter, more responsive to temperature changes, and more energy-efficient, but the wall-mounted heads are visible. Many lofts use a hybrid approach: rooftop unit for base heating, mini-splits for supplemental cooling and bedroom comfort.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list s-pills s-pills--glass\">\n<li>1 ton per 400-500 sq ft open floor plan<\/li>\n\n\n<li>R-6 insulation minimum on vertical ducts<\/li>\n\n\n<li>2-3 hour thermal lag in masonry walls<\/li>\n\n\n<li>70-80% energy recovery with ERVs<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Smart thermostat +20-30% savings<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--dark-gradient has-contrast-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-cta has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">HVAC that honors the architecture<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Keystone Comfort Co. specializes in HVAC systems for Easton&#039;s industrial loft conversions. We design ductwork that integrates with exposed ceilings, specify rooftop units sized for high-mass construction, and install zoning systems that match open-plan layouts. Every project balances aesthetics with performance\u2014your ductwork should look as intentional as your kitchen, and it should keep you comfortable year-round.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-buttons is-content-justification-center is-layout-flex wp-container-core-buttons-is-layout-35f06ea7 wp-block-buttons-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-button\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"\/contact\/\">Design Your Loft System<\/a><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading has-text-align-center wp-block-heading\">Our Work in Your Neighborhood<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-gallery alignwide has-nested-images columns-3 is-cropped s-g